<p>On this page you will find information about Measure R: A local sales tax ballot measure proposed in Rancho Mirage for November 2026.<br /><br /><strong>What is Measure R?<br /></strong>Measure R was referred to the ballot by the Rancho Mirage City Council. If approved by a majority of voters, it would establish a one cent transactions and use tax on taxable sales within the City, with revenue deposited into the City's General Fund. A "YES vote" approves the measure. A "NO vote" rejects the measure.</p>
<strong>Background</strong>
<p>The City of Rancho Mirage maintains local services that include Sheriff's patrols and crime prevention, fire protection and 9-1-1 emergency medical response, street and road maintenance, parks, the City's Library and Observatory, and other public facilities and programs. Public safety services are provided under contract with the Riverside County Sheriff's Department and CAL FIRE/Riverside County Fire.</p>
<p>Citing increased costs for provisioning of various City services, including public safety, and additional budget constraints, the Rancho Mirage City Council unanimously referred Measure R&nbsp; to the November 2026 ballot for consideration by City voters. It is a General Tax to fund the City&rsquo;s General Fund, and subject to simple majority vote by the voters of Rancho Mirage.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Key Facts About Measure R</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Referred to the November 3, 2026 ballot by the Rancho Mirage City Council on July 16, 2026, by a unanimous vote of 5-0.</li>
<li>The measure would establish a one cent (1%) transactions and use tax, commonly called a sales tax, on taxable sales within the City of Rancho Mirage.</li>
<li>The City's current sales tax rate is 7.75%. If the measure passes, the rate would be 8.75%, the same rate currently charged in Palm Desert, La Quinta, Indio, Coachella and Desert Hot Springs.</li>
<li>The tax would be paid by anyone making a taxable purchase in Rancho Mirage, including visitors. City estimates indicate that approximately 75% of sales tax dollars collected in Rancho Mirage are paid by tourists and visitors. Certain items are exempt from sales tax under state law, including most groceries, prescriptions and many medical supplies, rent, utilities and services.</li>
<li>The measure is a general tax. Revenue would be deposited in the City's General Fund for general government purposes and could be used for any lawful City purpose. By law these funds would have to remain in Rancho Mirage, can cannot be taken by the State or County.&nbsp;</li>
<li>City staff estimate the tax would generate approximately $7.5 million annually. Funds would remain in Rancho Mirage and could not be taken by the State or transferred to another agency.</li>
<li>The measure would remain in effect until ended by voters and would require public disclosure of spending.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rancho Mirage is a low property tax city, and an average of less than 4% of each&nbsp; property tax dollar collected within the City is allocated to the City's General Fund. The City's budgeted General Fund for the current fiscal year is $38.4 million, of which approximately 50% is devoted to public safety and approximately 12% to public works.</p>
<p><strong>What is the actual question that voters will be asked to decide?<br /><br /></strong><strong>Rancho Mirage Public Safety, Roads, City Services Measure<br /></strong><em>"RANCHO MIRAGE PUBLIC SAFETY, ROADS, CITY SERVICES MEASURE. Shall the measure maintaining Rancho Mirage's long-term financial stability, City services such as 9-1-1 emergency medical/fire response, fire protection, Sheriff's patrols, crime prevention/response programs; keeping public areas safe/clean; maintaining streets, roads, parks, libraries, fire stations; retaining/attracting local businesses, other services, by establishing a 1&cent; sales tax generating approximately $7,500,000 (seven point five million dollars) annually until ended by voters, requiring public spending disclosure, funds for Rancho Mirage only, be adopted?&nbsp; YES&nbsp; NO"</em></p>
<p><strong>How could Measure R funds be used?<br /></strong>Measure R revenue would be deposited into the City's General Fund. Funds could be used to support services that include:</p>
<ul>
<li>9-1-1 emergency medical and fire response</li>
<li>Fire protection</li>
<li>Sheriff's patrols and crime prevention and response programs</li>
<li>Maintaining streets, roads, sidewalks and parks</li>
<li>Maintaining libraries, fire stations and other City facilities</li>
<li>Retaining and attracting local businesses</li>
<li>Maintaining the City's long-term fiscal stability</li>
<li>Any other services and infrastructure maintained by the City&rsquo;s General Fund.</li>
</ul>
